After Dwight wore the tuxedo, did they then put it back on his grandfather to then rebury him in a barrel?
德怀特穿过那件燕尾服之后，他们是把它又穿回他祖父身上，然后把他装进桶里重新下葬了吗？

156
00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.660
Or does he just get to keep the tuxedo?
还是说他可以直接留下那件燕尾服？

157
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:07.460
I mean, Dwight mentions his grandfather was reburied in season three, episode four, Grief Counseling.
我是说，德怀特在第三季第四集《悲伤辅导》里提到过他祖父被重新下葬的事。

158
00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.780
I think they removed the tuxedo, kept the tuxedo, and put him in something else when they put him in the barrel.
我觉得他们应该是把燕尾服脱了下来，留下了它，然后在把祖父装进桶里时给他换了别的衣服。

159
00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:18.320
But he probably would have had the bullet holes in his suit.
但他的西装上应该会有弹孔才对。

160
00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.140
I think that's a very fine catch from Jeremy.
我觉得杰里米这个发现非常敏锐。

161
00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:21.780
Well done.
干得好。

162
00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.600
So now, Angela, I have something I'd like to point out that I cannot believe we did not discuss last time.
那么现在，安吉拉，有一点我想指出，真不敢相信我们上次竟然没讨论过。

163
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:35.280
At 15 minutes, 35 seconds, we have an amazing shot of Mindy's hair extensions.
在15分35秒处，有一个明迪接发的绝佳镜头。

164
00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:36.900
Full Mindy.
全套明迪接发。

165
00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:38.720
She's wearing a flower in her hair.
她头发上戴着一朵花。

166
00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:45.440
We've been literally talking about these extensions for episodes, and we failed to mention them in Casino Night.
我们已经连续好几集都在谈论这些接发了，结果在《赌场之夜》那一集里竟然漏掉了。

167
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.280
Here they are, everyone.
大家看，就在这儿。

168
00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.540
They are substantial extensions.
这些接发分量十足。

169
00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.520
Kelly has gone all out.
凯莉这次真是豁出去了。

170
00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.820
And I can't believe we didn't mention those.
真不敢相信我们竟然没提过这些。

171
00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:55.640
Okay.
好吧。

172
00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.500
Also, my friend, Jenna Fisher.
还有，我的朋友，珍娜·费舍尔。

173
00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:02.920
People say I say lady too much, so I'm going to start calling you my friend.
大家都说我太爱叫人“女士”了，所以我打算开始叫你“我的朋友”。

174
00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.100
I would like you to call me Jenna Fisher.
我希望你叫我珍娜·费舍尔。

175
00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.680
Oh, okay.
哦，好吧。

176
00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:08.640
My friend, Jenna Fisher.
我的朋友，珍娜·费舍尔。

177
00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:09.500
There we are.
这就对了。

178
00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:11.840
People also wrote in about Pam's hair.
还有人写信问到了帕姆的发型。

179
00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:18.720
Batya said, not a question, but I just have to say, Pam's hair looks so amazing throughout this entire episode.
巴蒂亚说，这不是个问题，但我必须得说，帕姆在这一整集里的发型看起来都太棒了。

180
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.440
And Melissa Lynn said, I bet there was another meeting about her hair.
梅丽莎·琳说，我敢打赌肯定又开过关于她发型的会议。

181
00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.560
Jenna, you know there was a meeting about that hair.
珍娜，你知道肯定开过关于那个发型的会议。

182
00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:27.460
Come on, give it up.
来吧，交代一下。

183
00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:35.580
There was a huge meeting about that hair, and we tried, again, as we did, several different options for Pam's hair.
确实开过一个关于那个发型的重大会议，我们像往常一样，为帕姆的发型尝试了多种方案。

184
00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:44.340
Ultimately, we decided to go half up, half down, but instead of just having Pam let her hair naturally dry into that kind of frizzy curl,
最终，我们决定采用半扎半披的发型，但帕姆并没有让头发自然风干成那种毛躁的卷发，

185
00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.000
she would go back in with a curling iron and kind of soften her curls.
而是用卷发棒重新打理了一下，让卷度变得柔和了一些。

186
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:55.980
We thought this was a good way for her to look like Pam, but like she'd given it a little effort, which seemed right on to me.
我们认为这样既能保持帕姆的风格，又显得她花了一点心思，我觉得这很合适。

187
00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:03.380
So she would have had to blow dry her hair out straight, then get a curling iron, and then put all those curls in, right?
所以她得先用吹风机把头发吹直，然后再用卷发棒卷出那些卷儿，对吧？

188
00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:04.300
No.
不对。

189
00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.000
She let her hair dry naturally and then went in with a curling iron and just sort of softened the curl that was there.
她是让头发自然风干，然后用卷发棒稍微柔和了一下原本的卷度。

190
00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:11.840
That's literally how we did that hairstyle.
那个发型真的是这么做出来的。

191
00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:13.400
We did not blow it out firSt.
我们并没有先把它吹直。

192
00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:14.180
Okay.
好吧。

193
00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:15.800
I just wanted to know.
我只是想了解一下。

194
00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:16.980
I was curious about that.
我对此很好奇。

195
00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:17.200
Yes.
是的。

196
00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:18.820
How many steps this was.
想知道这需要多少步骤。

197
00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:20.520
Well, here's one thing I should mention.
嗯，有一点我得提一下。

198
00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.040
One of the discussions was that whatever Pam's hair looked like, it had to look like she did it herself.
当时讨论的一个重点是，无论帕姆的发型是什么样，都必须看起来像是她自己打理的。

199
00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.820
It couldn't look like someone else did it for her, right?
不能看起来像是别人帮她弄的，对吧？

200
00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:40.500
And the truth is, I could have never, ever achieved that little swirly, if you look at the back of my head, I've got this swirly thing there.
说实话，我永远也弄不出那个小卷儿，如果你看我后脑勺的话，我那儿有个旋儿。

201
00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.360
It's a swirly bun thingy that holds up half the hair.
那是一个用来盘住一半头发的螺旋状发髻。

202
00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.640
Yes, because her Pam clip is not there.
没错，因为她的帕姆发夹没戴。

203
00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:46.660
There's no Pam clip.
根本没有帕姆发夹。

204
00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:50.020
It's a swirly thing with little pins.
那是个带小发针的螺旋状玩意儿。

205
00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:56.300
There's no, my arms couldn't bend in the way that would be necessary to achieve that.
不，我的胳膊根本弯不到能做出那种发型的角度。

206
00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.300
So, bit of a cheat.
所以，算是作弊吧。

207
00:09:58.780 --> 00:09:59.920
We went with it.
我们也就将就用了。

208
00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.180
Here's a little something for you guys listening.
给正在收听的各位准备了一点小福利。

209
00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:11.260
We always knew there was about to be a conversation about Pam's hair when Greg or one of the writers would come into hair and makeup because you never,
我们一直都知道，每当格雷格或编剧组的某个人走进化妆间时，肯定要讨论帕姆的发型，因为你从来、

210
00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:12.580
ever saw them in hair and makeup.
从来没见过他们出现在化妆间。

211
00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:16.860
You'd see them on set, but you would rarely see them come into hair and makeup.
你会在片场看到他们，但很少见他们走进化妆间。

212
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.760
So, whenever they did, we were like, oh, what's happening?
所以每当他们进来，我们就会想，噢，出什么事了？

213
00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.380
Either Pam has a new hairstyle or Kate's getting her head shaved.
要么是帕姆要换新发型了，要么是凯特要剃光头了。

214
00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:23.960
What's going on?
到底怎么了？

215
00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:30.740
Well, here's what I thought was so funny was the number of meetings we had about my hair in this episode.
嗯，我觉得特别好笑的是，这一集里我们为了我的发型开了多少次会。

216
00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:37.720
And all the while, Mindy was three chairs down from me getting long, luscious extensions that no one questioned.
而与此同时，明迪就在离我三个椅子远的地方接了一头又长又浓密的接发，却没人质疑过。

217
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:43.540
No one questioned how and why Kelly suddenly has long hair.
没人问过凯莉的头发怎么突然变长了，也没人问为什么。

218
00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:50.880
But we, you know, we curled my hair in a slightly new way and it was like seven meetings.
但我们呢，你知道，我们只是把我的头发稍微换了个卷法，就开了七次会。

219
00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:53.980
We were very protective of Pam's hair.
我们对帕姆的发型保护得非常严密。

220
00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:54.960
They really were.
确实如此。

221
00:10:55.220 --> 00:11:03.340
And good thing, too, because, well, I mean, every once in a while we messed up with, you know, Low Pony, but we've already spoken about that.
而且这样也好，因为，我是说，偶尔我们也会搞砸，比如那个“低马尾”，但我们已经聊过那个了。

222
00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:04.200
LP.
低马尾。

223
00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.020
We already covered LP.
我们已经聊过低马尾了。

224
00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:07.120
All right.
好吧。

225
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:08.940
I would like to talk about drink orders.
我想聊聊点酒的事。

226
00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:09.960
Attract it.
吸引它。

227
00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:11.020
Attract it.
吸引它。

228
00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:11.620
You try.
你试试。

229
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.160
And I want to talk about it.
而且我想聊聊这个。

230
00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:14.960
Here's the thing.
事情是这样的。

231
00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.480
At 20 minutes, three seconds, we see Jan and Carol.
在20分03秒处，我们看到了简和卡罗尔。

232
00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.840
They are side by side at the bar, which is totally awkward.
她们并排站在吧台前，这简直太尴尬了。

233
00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:21.500
OK.
好的。

234
00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:22.700
Guess what Carol ordered?
猜猜卡罗尔点了什么？

235
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:23.160
What?
什么？

236
00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:23.860
Red wine.
红酒。

237
00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:24.760
OK.
好的。

238
00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:26.220
Keeping it simple.
简单明了。

239
00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:26.820
Yeah.
是啊。

240
00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:27.080
Right?
对吧？

241
00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:27.700
Yeah.
没错。

242
00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.260
Jan orders a Cosmopolitan.
简点了一杯大都会鸡尾酒。

243
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:30.980
Mm.
嗯。

244
00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:36.920
You know, remember, Cosmopolitans were what I drank when I did my experiment with BJ Novak.
你知道，记得吗，大都会鸡尾酒就是我当年和BJ·诺瓦克做实验时喝的酒。

245
00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.080
About getting drunk for the Dundies.
关于为了邓迪奖颁奖礼而喝醉的那次。

246
00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.600
Remember, I told that story.
记得吗，我讲过那个故事。

247
00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:48.560
Is this because you would watch like one episode of Sex and the City and you're like, oh, people order Cosmopolitans when they go to a bar?
这是因为你看了《欲望都市》之类的剧，然后就想，噢，人们去酒吧时都会点大都会鸡尾酒吗？

248
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:50.260
Lady, that's literally correct.
女士，这完全正确。

249
00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:50.980
Yes.
是的。

250
00:11:51.440 --> 00:12:00.600
I had done so little drinking that when I had to order a drink, I said Cosmopolitan because it's what they drank on Sex and the City.
我当时几乎没怎么喝过酒，所以当我要点酒时，我说大都会鸡尾酒，因为那是她们在《欲望都市》里喝的酒。

251
00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:01.060
Yes.
是的。

252
00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:02.220
Yes.
没错。

253
00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.460
I didn't know any.
我一个都不知道。

254
00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:05.960
It was that or a screwdriver.
要么选那个，要么选螺丝刀。

255
00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.080
I knew those two drinks.
我就知道那两种酒。

256
00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:09.300
That's it.
就是这样。

257
00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:10.220
All right.
好吧。

258
00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:18.540
So Ryan comes up and orders a seven and seven with eight marachino cherries, sugar on the rim, blended if you can.
然后瑞安走过来，点了一杯七喜加威士忌，要加八颗马拉斯奇诺樱桃，杯口蘸糖，如果能打成冰沙就更好了。

259
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.260
And then doesn't Jim say, oh, that's still going on.
然后吉姆不是说了句，噢，那件事还在继续啊。

260
00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:23.280
That's still going on, huh?
那件事还在继续，是吧？

261
00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:24.120
Yes.
是的。

262
00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:24.960
Perfect line.
绝妙的台词。

263
00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:25.580
It's perfect.
太完美了。

264
00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:32.060
Well, at 14 minutes and 46 seconds, Michael goes up to Billy Merchant.
在14分46秒的时候，迈克尔走向了比利·默钱特。

265
00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.540
And we did not talk about this scene.
我们之前没讨论过这一幕。

266
00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:35.820
And I have some info here.
我这里有些信息。

267
00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:36.380
Okay.
好的。

268
00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:39.260
Michael says to Billy, your nurse is hot.
迈克尔对比利说，你的护士很辣。

269
00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.400
To which Billy says, she's not my nurse.
比利回答说，她不是我的护士。

270
00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:43.340
She's my girlfriend.
她是我女朋友。

271
00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:47.220
And Michael says, your nurse became your girlfriend?
迈克尔说，你的护士变成了你的女朋友？

272
00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:48.080
Sweet.
真不错。

273
00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.560
And Billy says, she was never my nurse.
比利说，她从来都不是我的护士。

274
00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.180
I met her at Chili's.
我是在奇利餐厅认识她的。

275
00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:54.580
All right.
好吧。

276
00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.040
We had someone write in.
有人给我们写信了。

277
00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:58.520
And I am so sorry, sir.
非常抱歉，先生。

278
00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.340
I lost your name.
我把你的名字弄丢了。

279
00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:00.920
Okay.
好吧。

280
00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.260
If you hear this, you know who you are.
如果你听到了，你应该知道你是谁。

281
00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.020
There's only one person who wrote in.
只有一个人写信过来。

282
00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.980
And I cannot, could not find the email again.
但我没能，没能再找到那封邮件。

283
00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:16.660
But they wrote in to say, is Billy Merchant's girlfriend the same woman who played the hostess Megan from Chili's in The Client?
但他们写信问，比利·默钱特的女朋友是不是就是《客户》那一集里奇利餐厅的接待员梅根？

284
00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:21.680
Remember in The Client, we loved how Michael kept remembering that the hostess name was Megan.
还记得在《客户》那一集里，我们很喜欢迈克尔一直记得接待员的名字叫梅根吗？

285
00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.080
He was like, Megan, Megan.
他当时一直说，梅根，梅根。

286
00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:23.640
Okay.
好的。

287
00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:25.220
Okay, no.
好的，不是。

288
00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:28.860
It is not Megan from Chili's, from The Client.
她不是《客户》里奇利餐厅的梅根。

289
00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:35.780
But it is the same actress who played the waitress at Chili's in Dundies.
但她确实是《邓迪奖》那一集里奇利餐厅的同一位女服务员演员。

290
00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:36.680
What?
什么？

291
00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.420
And her name is Amanda Warren.
她的名字叫阿曼达·沃伦。

292
00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.400
This actress, Amanda Warren, she was a background performer in the Dundies episode.
这位演员阿曼达·沃伦，她在《邓迪奖》那一集中是一名背景演员。

293
00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:45.720
She played a waitress.
她扮演了一名服务员。

294
00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:51.560
And we hired the same actress to play Billy Merchant's girlfriend in Casino Night.
我们又雇了同一位演员在《赌场之夜》里扮演比利·默钱特的女朋友。

295
00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:53.140
That's some good continuity.
这连续性做得真好。

296
00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:54.700
Good continuity.
很好的连续性。

297
00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:55.920
Come on.
得了吧。

298
00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:57.100
Some good continuity.
确实是很棒的连续性。

299
00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:03.980
And by the way, thank you, Kentopedia, for doing that deep dive on our casting file to get me the answer to that question.
顺便说一句，谢谢肯托百科，帮我深入挖掘了我们的选角档案，才让我得到了这个问题的答案。

300
00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:05.540
Aw, Kentopedia.
噢，肯托百科。

301
00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:11.980
Well, at 15 minutes, 51 seconds, Michael sits down at the poker table to play No Limit Texas Hold'em.
在15分51秒时，迈克尔坐在扑克桌前玩无限注德州扑克。

302
00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.980
Michael goes all in against Toby.
迈克尔对托比全押了。

303
00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:16.180
Toby calls.
托比跟注。

304
00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:18.460
Michael gets really mad.
迈克尔非常生气。

305
00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:24.300
Toby shows a pair of jacks and then Michael folds his cards and storms away from the table.
托比亮出一对J，迈克尔随后弃牌，气冲冲地离开了牌桌。

306
00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.560
We have a fan question from Kay Guth.
我们收到一个来自凯·古斯的粉丝提问。

307
00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:36.980
As a poker player, did it bother Jenna that when Toby calls Michael's all in, Michael just leaves even though he could still win?
作为一名扑克玩家，珍娜，当托比跟注迈克尔的全押时，迈克尔明明还有获胜机会却直接离开，这让你感到困扰吗？

308
00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:38.880
Yes.
是的。

309
00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:41.200
Yes, Kay Guth.
是的，凯·古斯。

310
00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.360
Yes, it bothered me a lot.
是的，这让我非常困扰。

311
00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:50.360
Also, in the script, it's said that Michael turns over his cards, revealing a 9 and a 10,
另外，剧本里说迈克尔翻开了他的牌，是一张9和一张10，

312
00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:55.500
which means he could have won by landing a straight, right?
这意味着他本可以通过凑成顺子获胜，对吧？

313
00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.120
If he gets a straight, that's going to beat Toby's two jacks.
如果他凑成顺子，就能赢过托比的一对J。

314
00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:00.260
Here's the thing.
问题就在这里。

315
00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:01.940
You never walk away from the table.
你永远不该离开牌桌。

316
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:05.000
In the scene, Michael doesn't reveal his cards.
在这一幕里，迈克尔并没有亮出他的牌。

317
00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:06.300
He just folds his cards.
他只是弃牌了。

318
00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.960
So I guess we're left to presume that they were really awful.
所以我猜我们只能假设他的牌真的很烂。

319
00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:13.240
But you never know what you're going to get on the flop, on the turn, and the river.
但你永远不知道翻牌、转牌和河牌会出什么。

320
00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:15.180
You never walk away from your hand.
你永远不该放弃你的手牌。

321
00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:16.240
Thank you, Kay Guth.
谢谢你，凯·古斯。

322
00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:17.120
It did bother me.
这确实让我困扰。

323
00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.640
Kay Guth, none of that made sense to me.
凯·古斯，刚才那些话我一点都没听懂。

324
00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:22.080
None of what Jenna said made sense to me.
珍娜说的那些我一点都没听懂。

325
00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.980
But I'll tell you what I'm going to take away from it.
但我会告诉你我从中领悟到了什么。

326
00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.200
I am not walking away from the table.
我绝不会离开牌桌。

327
00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:27.440
There you go.
这就对了。

328
00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:28.160
Thank you.
谢谢。

329
00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:35.880
Angela, at 20 minutes, 23 seconds, I don't think we discussed Creed's stealing of the poker chips,
安吉拉，在20分23秒时，我觉得我们还没讨论过克里德偷筹码的事，

330
00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:37.620
which becomes a bit of a runner.
这成了一个贯穿始终的梗。

331
00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:40.140
Yeah, it's a total runner throughout the episode.
没错，这确实是整集里的一个贯穿梗。

332
00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:45.080
And as far as I can tell, Angela Martin is the only person that really clocks it, right?
据我观察，安吉拉·马丁是唯一真正注意到这一点的人，对吧？

333
00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:47.260
Yeah, but then you don't say anything.
是的，但你什么也没说。

334
00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:48.660
I don't say anything.
我什么也没说。

335
00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.020
Isn't that interesting?
这难道不有趣吗？

336
00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:54.540
Angela, do you think that your character, because your character clocks a lot of things,
安吉拉，你觉得你的角色——因为你的角色注意到了很多事情——

337
00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.280
do you keep a little file?
你会专门建个档案吗？

338
00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:57.640
Oh, I think.
哦，我觉得会。

339
00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.780
Do you think maybe you file that away somewhere?
你觉得你会把这些事归档到什么地方吗？

340
00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:01.420
A hundred percent.
百分之百会。

341
00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:02.260
For use later?
为了以后使用？

342
00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.020
I think that's one of the things Dwight and I have in common.
我觉得这是德怀特和我共同点之一。

343
00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:08.340
He's tracking all kinds of stuff, and so am I.
他在追踪各种各样的事情，我也是。

344
00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:11.520
And so when the two of us think that we're going to have power together, we're like,
所以当我们俩觉得我们能联手掌握权力时，我们就会觉得，

345
00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:13.820
oh, we own these people.
哦，这些人都在我们掌控之中。

346
00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.120
Oh, you guys definitely have some secret files.
哦，你们俩肯定有一些秘密档案。

347
00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:17.600
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

348
00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:18.380
For sure.
肯定的。

349
00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:23.880
Well, lady, I think we should bring Ken on, because now I have questions for him.
好吧，女士，我觉得我们该请肯上场了，因为现在我有问题要问他。

350
00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:24.420
Same.
我也是。

351
00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:25.520
Here's what we're going to do.
我们接下来要这样做。

352
00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:30.000
We'll take a break, and then we will be back with the director of Casino Night and many other
我们要休息一下，稍后回来，届时将请到《赌场之夜》以及其他许多剧集的导演，肯·夸帕斯。

353
00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.520
episodes, Ken Cuapas.
肯·夸帕斯。

354
00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:32.140
Go ahead.
请继续。

355
00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:33.420
And go ahead to break.
那就先休息一下吧。

356
00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:44.690
Hello!
你好！

357
00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:49.600
So thrilled to have you.
很高兴你能来。

358
00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:52.800
I am so happy to be here.
我很高兴能来到这里。

359
00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.020
Ken, you and I share a hometown.
肯，你和我是老乡。

360
00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:59.600
We do, and I have a very distinct memory about one of our first meetings.
确实，我对我们第一次见面时的情景记忆犹新。

361
00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:03.060
So I went to an all-boys high school in St. Louis.
我当时在圣路易斯上的一所全男校。

362
00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:05.220
St. Louis University High School.
圣路易斯大学附属高中。

363
00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:11.520
All boys, and the only girls I met in high school came from two sister schools,
全都是男生，我在高中唯一认识的女生来自两所姐妹学校，

364
00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:15.100
Ursuline Academy and Narenx.
乌苏林学院和纳伦克斯高中。

365
00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:18.980
And those were the girls who were actors in our plays.
她们就是我们话剧里的女演员。

366
00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.820
So anyways, when you and I auditioned, I remember having this moment where I thought,
总之，当你我参加试镜时，我记得当时心里想，

367
00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:29.540
wow, this woman has such a weird Catholic girl vibe going on here.
哇，这个女人身上有一种很奇特的教会女校女生的气质。

368
00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:36.040
She reminds me of the girls I knew from Narenx High School, and that's where you went.
她让我想起了我在纳伦克斯高中认识的那些女生，而你就是那所学校毕业的。

369
00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:37.460
That's where I went to school!
我确实是那所学校毕业的！

370
00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:38.520
Yes!
没错！

371
00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:44.720
And the only boys I knew came from St. Louis University High School to be in our place.
而我认识的唯一男生就是从圣路易斯大学附属高中来参加我们话剧演出的那些人。

372
00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.220
No, it was the weirdest, most random thing.
不，这真是最奇怪、最巧合的事情了。

373
00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:55.340
But obviously, there's an important St. Louis contingent on the show, obviously.
但很明显，剧组里有一个重要的圣路易斯小团体，显而易见。

374
00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:56.840
There's a huge one!
确实有一个很大的团体！

375
00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:59.280
It's you guys and Ellie and Phyllis.
就是你们，还有艾莉和菲利斯。

376
00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:00.600
Am I missing anyone else?
我还有漏掉谁吗？

377
00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:02.840
Rusty Mahmood, our first AD.
拉斯蒂·马哈茂德，我们的第一副导演。

378
00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:03.320
Oh, Rusty!
噢，拉斯蒂！

379
00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:04.200
Oh my gosh.
天哪。

380
00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:04.940
Yeah.
是啊。

381
00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:10.620
I really wasn't aware of how many people were from St. Louis until the World Series that
我真的没意识到有多少人来自圣路易斯，直到那一季大家都在看世界大赛的时候，

382
00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:12.580
one season when everyone was watching.
那一季大家都在看。

383
00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:13.080
Remember?
还记得吗？

384
00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:16.320
It was like, I'm going to get it wrong.
当时就像是，我可能会记错。

385
00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:20.120
But anyway, I just remember you guys were really intense about baseball.
但不管怎样，我只记得你们对棒球非常狂热。

386
00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:21.100
And I was like, why?
当时我就想，为什么？

387
00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:22.900
And you guys were all from St. Louis.
原来你们都是圣路易斯人。

388
00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:23.440
Right.
没错。

389
00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:26.140
It was a bad World Series that year for the Cardinals.
那一年红雀队的世界大赛打得很糟糕。

390
00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:27.500
Oh, is that why you lost?
噢，所以你们才输了吗？

391
00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:27.960
You're so quiet.
你们怎么这么安静。

392
00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:28.660
I'm sorry.
抱歉。

393
00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.200
I thought I was bringing up a really great memory.
我本以为我提起的是一段很美好的回忆。

394
00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:32.720
You guys were like silent.
你们当时就像沉默了一样。

395
00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:33.520
I'm like, what's happening?
我在想，发生什么事了？

396
00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:33.980
No.
没有。

397
00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:35.180
Okay, sorry.
好吧，抱歉。

398
00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:35.780
Okay, sorry.
好吧，抱歉。

399
00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:36.540
Okay.
好吧。

400
00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:43.420
Well, Ken, we always like to ask our guests how they came to be a part of The Office.
那么，肯，我们总是喜欢问嘉宾是如何加入《办公室》剧组的。

401
00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:45.580
And you were our first director.
而且你是我们的第一位导演。

402
00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:46.900
You directed our pilot.
你执导了我们的试播集。

403
00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:48.920
We'd love to hear how that happened.
我们很想听听那是怎么发生的。

404
00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:52.700
Well, I did not know Greg before the show.
嗯，在拍这部剧之前我不认识格雷格。

405
00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.180
I met with him.
我和他见过面。

406
00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:55.640
I had an interview with him.
我接受过他的面试。

407
00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:59.200
I was actually very nervous to meet him because I knew his work.
其实去见他时我非常紧张，因为我了解他的作品。

408
00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:00.300
I really admired him.
我真的很钦佩他。

409
00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:09.580
And I also was nervous because, like, so many people said, this is a terrible idea to remake this show, The Office.
而且我也很紧张，因为当时很多人都说，翻拍《办公室》这部剧是个糟糕的主意。

410
00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:15.940
And so I went into this job interview with all these different feelings, including, like, you know, what if I get this job?
所以我带着各种复杂的心情去参加了面试，包括，你知道的，万一我被录用了怎么办？

411
00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:20.000
This is an impossible task, remaking this beloved show.
翻拍这部备受喜爱的剧集是一项不可能完成的任务。

412
00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:27.340
And I don't remember much of the meaning except for one thing, and that is Greg asked me what I thought of the British show.
我不记得面试的大部分内容了，只记得一件事，那就是格雷格问我对英版剧集有什么看法。

413
00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:36.060
I said I really loved it, but that I was completely confused about the layout of the paper company in the British show.
我说我真的很喜欢，但我完全搞不懂英版剧集里那家造纸公司的布局。

414
00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:40.680
I just found it totally – I couldn't figure out where, you know, where people sat or anything like that.
我就是觉得完全——我根本弄不清楚人们坐在哪里，或者诸如此类的事情。

415
00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:45.640
And I remember even in the meeting thinking, this is a really bad move on my part.
我记得当时在会上我就想，我这么说真是太糟糕了。

416
00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:51.760
You know, like, in theory, you hire a director because they have, I don't know, visual sense.
你知道，理论上讲，你雇佣一位导演是因为他们有，我不知道，视觉感官之类的东西。

417
00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:55.340
And here I was basically saying I'm, like, spatially challenged.
而我当时基本上是在说，我在空间感上是有障碍的。

418
00:19:56.100 --> 00:20:02.520
And Greg, to my, you know, delight, he goes, oh, yeah, I'm totally confused about that, too.
让我高兴的是，格雷格说，哦，是啊，我也完全搞不懂那个布局。

419
00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:09.420
And then we were sitting – I don't know where the meetings took place, but we were sitting on a sofa.
然后我们坐在——我不记得会议是在哪里举行的，但我们当时坐在沙发上。

420
00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:15.400
And then we kind of scrunched down next to the coffee table and got pieces of paper and pens and started, like, drawing.
我们缩在咖啡桌旁，拿了纸和笔开始画了起来。

421
00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:16.600
Like, we were a couple of kids.
就像两个孩子一样。

422
00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:22.320
We were, like, drawing what we imagined the original paper company layout was like.
我们就像是在画我们想象中原版造纸公司的布局。

423
00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.700
So that was, I think, the turning point of the meeting.
所以我想，那应该是会议的转折点。

424
00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:26.940
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

425
00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.660
I love that story.
我喜欢这个故事。

426
00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:36.060
I guess, you know, it's a little bit the lesson of, you know, sometimes it's okay just to admit what you don't know, I guess.
我想，这或许也算是一个教训，有时候承认自己不懂也没什么大不了的。

427
00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:41.900
It really speaks to Greg, too, because I could see Greg getting a bunch of paper and saying, let's draw it.
这也真的很符合格雷格的风格，因为我能想象格雷格拿出一堆纸说，我们把它画出来吧。

428
00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:43.080
Let's figure it out.
我们把它搞清楚。

429
00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:44.800
Oh, that's exactly what it was.
哦，事实确实如此。

430
00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:47.800
The two of us just, like, little kids drawing for a while.
我们俩就像小孩子一样画了一会儿。

431
00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:53.700
So when you got the job then, how did you approach building the Dunder Mifflin world?
那么当你得到这份工作后，你是如何着手构建邓德·米夫林（Dunder Mifflin）的世界的？

432
00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:56.280
Did you scout real offices?
你有去考察过真实的办公室吗？

433
00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:59.760
Because, you know, our first location was that real office in Culver City.
因为，你知道，我们的第一个拍摄地点就是卡尔弗城那个真实的办公室。

434
00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.600
How did you choose your space?
你是如何选择空间的？

435
00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:09.400
Well, the space, as I recall, the office suite that we shot in was just this kind of shuttered office.
嗯，关于那个空间，我记得我们拍摄的办公套间只是那种闲置的办公室。

436
00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:11.240
You know, there was nothing going on.
你知道，里面什么都没有。

437
00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.560
It was basically a big, empty rectangle.
它基本上就是一个巨大的空长方形。

438
00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:14.940
There was nothing in it, really.
里面真的什么都没有。

439
00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:22.960
And I remember a lot of questions that Greg and I, you know, batted back and forth, including, should there be dividers between the desks?
我记得格雷格和我反复讨论过很多问题，包括，办公桌之间是否应该有隔板？

440
00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:24.340
Should there be cubicles?
是否应该有隔间？

441
00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:31.680
And, you know, if there are dividers, should they be low enough that people could still kind of see each other over them?
而且，你知道，如果有隔板的话，它们是否应该低到人们仍然可以越过它们看到彼此？

442
00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:37.700
And we decided, you know, happily to have no dividers except accounting.
我们最终决定，除了会计部，不设任何隔板。

443
00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:38.540
And accounting.
还有会计部。

444
00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:44.480
And which is also a very bizarre divider because you're still staring at each other.
那也是一个非常奇怪的隔断，因为你们依然在盯着对方看。

445
00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:46.540
Well, you know I have my theory.
嗯，你知道我有我的理论。

446
00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:54.440
I have my theory that Angela Martin requested that because she needed a buffer between her and Kevin because he's, you know, he's a little smelly.
我的理论是安吉拉·马丁要求那样做的，因为她需要一个缓冲带，把她和凯文隔开，因为他，你知道，他有点味道。

447
00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:02.440
I remember even when we decided it would be glass, it was like, well, what's the point of having that divider?
我记得即使在我们决定用玻璃隔断时，我还在想，设那个隔断有什么意义呢？

448
00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:08.200
But I think it was just sort of we wanted some sense that somebody was in a cubicle-y kind of thing.
但我认为我们只是想营造一种感觉，让人觉得那是某种隔间。

449
00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:16.800
But I do remember spending a lot of time thinking about the relationship between Pam's chair and Jim's chair.
但我确实记得花了很多时间思考帕姆的椅子和吉姆的椅子之间的关系。

450
00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:27.600
That actually was something that I played around a bunch with different ways that, I mean, Pam's, you know, your reception area was always where it was.
那确实是我反复琢磨过很多次的事情，我是说，帕姆的，你知道，你的接待区位置一直没变。

451
00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:30.580
But it was a question of where would Jim sit?
但问题是，吉姆该坐在哪儿呢？

452
00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:34.860
And I, you know, and it seems now like how could it not be what it was?
而且我，你知道，现在看来，怎么可能不是那样呢？

453
00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:43.200
But I love the idea that you always look at Jim and Jim has to turn to look at you.
但我很喜欢这个设定：你总是看着吉姆，而吉姆必须转过头来看你。

454
00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:50.040
It's like the simplest thing, but I thought that somehow the way your desk's related would help tell the Pam-Jim story.
这看起来很简单，但我认为你们办公桌的这种相对位置，在某种程度上确实有助于讲述帕姆和吉姆的故事。

455
00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:59.840
And so I love, nothing makes me happier than some of the shots we did where like John's in profile in the foreground and you're in the background, Jenna, like gazing at him.
所以我很喜欢，没有什么比我们拍的那些镜头更让我开心的了，比如约翰在前景侧身，而你在背景里，珍娜，深情地注视着他。

456
00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:08.360
And he either is unaware that you're looking at him or he's completely pretending to be unaware and he knows very well that you're looking at him.
他要么是没察觉到你在看他，要么就是完全装作没察觉，其实他心里很清楚你在看他。

457
00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:15.440
So I just feel like that was actually something that was like a key moment in setting up the Dunder Mifflin world.
所以我感觉这确实是构建邓德·米夫林世界的一个关键时刻。

458
00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:16.900
I love that.
我喜欢这个。

459
00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:17.400
Sure.
当然。

460
00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:21.720
And that would have been totally different if Jim sat where Dwight sits.
如果吉姆坐在德怀特的位置上，那一切就完全不同了。

461
00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:24.920
Yeah, then they'd be staring at each other all the time.
没错，那样他们就会一直盯着对方看了。

462
00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:26.300
Oh, I love that.
噢，我喜欢那样。

463
00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:35.700
And the other thing, you know, I was thinking about our, you know, morning 30-minute exercise where, you know, we, you know, that actually,
还有一件事，你知道，我在想我们那个，你知道，每天30分钟的练习，你知道，我们，你知道，那其实，

464
00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:41.680
that exercise was inspired by Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.
那个练习是受瑞奇·热维斯和斯蒂芬·莫昌特的启发。

465
00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:49.100
And they said that when they shot the British show that every morning or occasionally they would just stop and do what they called general views.
他们说在拍摄英版剧集时，每天早上或者偶尔，他们会停下来拍一些他们称之为“全景”的镜头。

466
00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:52.100
And they were basically different establishing shots.
基本上就是一些不同的定场镜头。

467
00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:59.740
And so when we did it, I didn't really think about it at the time, but I really feel like it sort of set the tone for the day.
所以当我们这样做的时候，当时我没多想，但我真的觉得这为一天定下了基调。

468
00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:10.340
And that once we kind of segued into a scripted scene after, you know, a half hour of shooting shots of, I don't know, you know, like Angela, you, you know,
而且一旦我们在拍摄了半小时的镜头之后转入剧本场景，我不知道，你知道，比如安吉拉，你，你知道，

469
00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:12.480
doodling a picture of a cat or something.
在涂鸦画一只猫之类的。

470
00:24:12.780 --> 00:24:20.180
Like when we segued into a scripted scene, everyone kind of maintained that sense of just being like under observation.
就像当我们转入剧本场景时，每个人都保持着那种仿佛处于被观察状态的感觉。

471
00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:23.200
You know, nobody was doing a scene.
你知道，没人是在演戏。

472
00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:32.800
And it was, it was, that's, you know, everyone was still sort of being, I was still just eavesdropping on these characters as opposed to, oh, let's do a scene.
那是，那是，你知道，每个人都还是处于那种状态，我依然只是在偷听这些角色的对话，而不是说“好，我们来演一场戏”。

473
00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:38.840
You know, the other thing that was great, I mean, none of the phones on the set worked, obviously.
你知道，还有一点很棒，我是说，片场所有的电话显然都不能用。

474
00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:45.120
They were all dummy phones, but it kind of gave some of the cast members who didn't have dialogue in the pilot, like Phyllis didn't have,
它们都是模型电话，但这给了那些在试播集中没有台词的演员一些机会，比如菲利斯，她没有，

475
00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:47.260
doesn't have a line of dialogue in the pilot.
在试播集中没有一句台词。

476
00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:54.340
But when we were doing our morning exercise, she was like on this fake phone improvising a sales call.
但当我们做晨间练习时，她会对着那个假电话即兴表演销售通话。

477
00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:56.680
And so I remember actually filming her.
所以我记得我确实拍了她。

478
00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:05.080
And so it was kind of a great way to, for all of the cast members to start to find their voice, even if they didn't have words in the script.
所以这对所有演员来说，都是一种开始寻找自己角色定位的好方法，即使他们在剧本里没有台词。

479
00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:17.080
Well, Ken, one of the things I remember as well, just was watching you and our camera operator watching you and Randall sort of like scope out the look of the scene.
嗯，肯，我记得的另一件事，就是看着你和我们的摄影师，看着你和兰德尔在观察场景的视觉效果。

480
00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:27.360
And we would rehearse for the crew and you guys would watch and your eyes dart back and forth, back and forth from who's talking.
我们会为剧组排练，你们就在旁边看，眼神在说话的人之间来回扫视。

481
00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:35.280
And you're looking around and I would find myself, if I didn't have lines in the scene, I would only watch you.
你们四处张望，而我会发现，如果我在那个场景里没有台词，我只会盯着你们看。

482
00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:43.900
I would watch you watching the scene, figuring out where you wanted the camera to be and how you wanted to tell the story.
我会看着你们观察场景，琢磨你们想把摄像机放在哪儿，以及想如何讲述这个故事。

483
00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:46.640
Well, I can't speak for other directors.
嗯，我不能代表其他导演。

484
00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:50.240
And obviously the show, there's so many great directors who worked on the show.
显然这部剧有很多优秀的导演参与过。

485
00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:56.540
But for me, it was always critical to stand next to the camera, not to be off watching at a monitor.
但对我来说，站在摄像机旁边总是至关重要的，而不是躲在监视器后面看。

486
00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:04.480
And part of what excited me was because I was standing next to the camera, I could see everyone who wasn't on camera.
让我兴奋的部分原因在于，因为我站在摄像机旁边，我能看到所有不在镜头里的人。

487
00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:12.040
And so, for instance, like Jenna, if we were looking at you, but Angela, I caught something that you were doing out of the corner of my eye.
所以，举个例子，珍娜，如果我们正拍着你，但我用余光捕捉到了安吉拉你在做些什么。

488
00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:16.120
If we did another take, I might say, you know, find Angela at some point.
如果我们再拍一条，我可能会说，你知道，找个机会拍拍安吉拉。

489
00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:21.320
So it just gave me a sense of where we might look.
所以这让我对我们可能关注的地方有了一种感觉。

490
00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:28.380
I mean, Greg put it really well when he was on the podcast, when he talked about Booze Cruise.
我是说，格雷格在播客上谈到《酒水游轮》那一集时说得非常好。

491
00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:36.900
And he talked about, I mean, among Greg's many talents is the way he sort of empowers the camera operator.
他谈到了，我是说，格雷格的众多天赋之一就是他赋予摄影师权力的方式。

492
00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:45.900
And he, you know, he's sort of encouraging the operator to kind of get curious about what's going on, to look for things, to find things, to kind of get interested.
他，你知道，他会鼓励摄影师对正在发生的事情产生好奇，去寻找事物，去发现事物，去产生兴趣。

493
00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:53.160
And I think that's such a, I mean, it's such a fantastic way to approach the camera operating.
我认为这是一种，我是说，这是一种非常棒的摄像方式。

494
00:26:53.420 --> 00:27:00.120
But for me, at the beginning, it was mostly I just needed to figure out what else could the camera find take to take.
但对我来说，起初我主要是需要弄清楚摄像机还能拍到什么。

495
00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:04.080
Anyways, that's a long and powerful way of answering the question.
总之，这是一个漫长而有力的回答。

496
00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:05.400
No, I loved that.
不，我很喜欢这个。

497
00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:10.920
The other thing I loved was the idea that the camera would be, quote, in the wrong place.
我喜欢的另一件事是摄像机“在错误位置”的构思。

498
00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:15.460
So we didn't do this a lot as time went on.
随着时间推移，我们没怎么再这样做了。

499
00:27:15.620 --> 00:27:24.840
But at the beginning, there were a few times where, for instance, like, Jenna, if I was on you, but Angela, you had a line.
但在开始的时候，有几次，比如，珍娜，如果我在拍你，但安吉拉，你有台词。

500
00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:28.640
And then we'd whip over, but we'd get to you too late, Angela.
然后我们会快速转过去，但我们转到你那儿太晚了，安吉拉。

501
00:27:28.780 --> 00:27:32.540
So we'd arrive just for like a dead, bit of dead air.
所以我们到的时候，刚好会有一段冷场。

502
00:27:33.260 --> 00:27:35.420
So we'd get there, wouldn't get the line.
我们到了那里，却没能接上戏。

503
00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:40.580
So occasionally it was great to kind of choreograph some of those missteps a little bit.
所以偶尔能把那些失误稍微编排一下，感觉真的很棒。

504
00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:48.080
There's a lot of great times where it just feels like deliberately the camera's not quite ready for what's about to happen.
有很多精彩的时刻，感觉就像是摄像机故意没准备好迎接即将发生的事情一样。

505
00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:55.120
I was looking at Diversity Day again because I wanted to see, you know, Pam falling asleep on Jim's shoulder,
我重温了《多元日》那一集，因为我想看看，你知道的，帕姆靠在吉姆肩膀上睡着的那一幕，

506
00:27:55.480 --> 00:27:59.420
which may be like my favorite scene that I directed in the whole series.
那可能是我在整个系列中执导的最喜欢的一场戏。

507
00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:00.600
I love that scene so much.
我太喜欢那场戏了。

508
00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:11.400
But what's nice about it is, and I'm sure it's Randall, that when, Jenna, when you lean over onto John's shoulder, we're not quite framed for you.
但它的妙处在于——我肯定那是兰德尔（摄影师）的手笔——珍娜，当你靠在约翰肩膀上时，我们的取景并没有完全对准你。

509
00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:15.640
And so suddenly the camera sort of like quickly zooms into a tighter shot.
所以摄像机突然快速变焦，拉近了镜头。

510
00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:21.340
But at the beginning, it's kind of just a kind of a loose frame with the two of you and maybe a couple other people.
但一开始，那只是一个比较松散的构图，画面里有你们俩，可能还有另外几个人。

511
00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:22.040
Yeah.
没错。

512
00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:26.620
I wanted to ask you, Ken, about marks on the ground.
肯，我想问你关于地面标记的事。

513
00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:28.960
So when I came out to L.A.
我刚到洛杉矶的时候，

514
00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:33.300
and I had my theater degree, I took a bunch of on-camera acting classes.
拿着戏剧学位，上了很多镜头前表演课。

515
00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:36.660
And the big thing I learned was how to hit your mark.
我学到的最重要的一点就是如何找准机位标记。

516
00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:51.040
And a mark is a piece of tape on the ground that the actor must walk to and stand on because the camera has meticulously measured the distance from the mark to the camera so that everybody's in focus.
标记就是贴在地上的一块胶带，演员必须走到上面站好，因为摄像机已经精确测量了标记到镜头的距离，这样每个人都在焦点内。

517
00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:56.380
And, like, hitting your mark was a thing that I was constantly nervous about as an actor.
作为一名演员，我总是很担心能不能找准标记。

518
00:28:56.380 --> 00:29:00.740
And I remember coming in on the pilot and asking, where is my mark?
我记得拍试播集时，我问：我的标记在哪里？

519
00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:05.460
Because I'm now a professional actress on a television show, and I must hit my mark.
因为我现在是电视剧里的专业演员了，我必须找准我的标记。

520
00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:08.040
Like, it's the one thing I learned.
这是我学到的唯一一件事。

521
00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:10.380
This is what I learned from my classes.
这就是我从课上学到的。

522
00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:12.620
Ask where your mark is.
问清楚你的标记在哪里。

523
00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:14.740
So I'm like, where is my mark?
所以我问：我的标记在哪里？

524
00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:16.200
And you said no marks.
而你说没有标记。

525
00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.000
The actor shall have no marks.
演员不应该有标记。

526
00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:19.940
And you're like, what?
你当时就像是，什么？

527
00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:20.720
No marks?
没有标记？

528
00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:23.740
What did I spend that $350 on?
那我那350美元花在什么上了？

529
00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:25.540
Well, wait, I have a question, though.
等等，我有个问题。

530
00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:30.300
Did it feel weird to not know where you were supposed to stand at times?
有时候不知道自己该站在哪里，会不会觉得很奇怪？

531
00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:35.360
And it was such a relief because it freed me up.
那真是一种解脱，因为它让我彻底放松了。

532
00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:44.100
I had started every scene as an actor on camera not thinking about where did I just come from?
以前作为镜头前演员，我开始每一场戏时，想的都不是我刚从哪儿来？

533
00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:45.620
What's my emotional life?
我的情感状态是什么？

534
00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:46.980
What am I doing in the scene?
我在戏里要做什么？

535
00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:49.880
I started every scene with, am I standing on my mark?
我开始每一场戏时想的都是：我站到标记上了吗？

536
00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:52.460
Like, that's what was going through my head was, did I stand on it properly?
我脑子里想的就是：我站得对吗？

537
00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:53.380
I think I'm off.
我觉得我偏了。

538
00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:54.380
Should I look down?
我要低头看吗？

539
00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:55.360
I looked down.
我低头看了。

540
00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:56.180
Did it look obvious?
看起来很明显吗？

541
00:29:56.180 --> 00:30:01.400
So it was so wonderful that I wasn't worried about where I was standing.
所以不用担心自己站在哪里，这种感觉太棒了。

542
00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:04.920
You would just say, just land in this general area.
你只会说，大概在这个区域停下就行。

543
00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:09.220
You know, walk over to Jim's desk somewhere between here and here.
你知道，走到吉姆桌子旁，就在这儿到那儿之间。

544
00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:11.400
Wherever you land, we'll find you.
无论你停在哪儿，我们都会找到你。

545
00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:16.060
An important part of that is that we had a lighting plan that, for the most part,
其中一个重要的部分是，我们的灯光方案在很大程度上，

546
00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:21.100
meant that you could land anywhere and you would be, you know, equally lit.
意味着你可以停在任何地方，而且你受到的光照都是一样的。

547
00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:21.540
Right.
没错。

548
00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:23.120
That's actually, that's the key.
那其实才是关键。

549
00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:28.280
I mean, and I mean, I would say, I mean, there's nothing against marks because if we're doing
我是说，我并不是反对标记，因为如果我们正在拍

550
00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:33.220
a scene, you know, where we have some complicated lighting going on, you, you do, you know,
一场戏，你知道，涉及到复杂的灯光布置时，你确实，你知道，

551
00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:35.720
occasionally need to actually hit the right spot.
偶尔确实需要精准地捕捉到那个点。

552
00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:39.220
Yeah, you can't, you can't be too far off or there will be no light on you.
没错，你不能偏离太远，否则光线就照不到你了。

553
00:30:39.460 --> 00:30:39.800
Yeah.
是的。

554
00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:41.680
But I think that that was part of it.
但我认为那是其中的一部分原因。

555
00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:49.160
But the other part of it was just the idea that generally that we were never staging things
而另一部分原因仅仅是基于这样一个理念：我们从来不是为了摄像机

556
00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:50.060
for the camera.
而去刻意安排场景。

557
00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:52.660
Well, we were, we were always staging things for the camera.
好吧，我们确实一直都是为了摄像机在安排场景。

558
00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:57.200
